{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Mean total biomass per transect increases with depth stratum, indicating depth‐dependent biomass distribution on reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify how total biomass measured in each transect varies across depth strata.",  
      "steps":"1. From reef.csv, compute total biomass per transect (sum of biomass for each transect record).  2. Ensure each transect record has depth_stratum (e.g. shallow vs deep) and id_reef and year.  3. Fit a linear mixed‐effects model: biomass_per_transect ~ depth_stratum + (1|id_reef) + (1|year).  4. Test whether the coefficient for depth_stratum is positive (deeper strata have higher mean biomass).  5. Visualize estimated marginal means of biomass_per_transect by depth_stratum with confidence intervals.","deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary table with depth_stratum coefficient.  2. Bar or point plot of average biomass_per_transect by depth_stratum.  3. Interpretation of depth effect on transect biomass."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Reefs with larger year-to-year changes in total biomass exhibit higher species turnover (Jaccard dissimilarity) between consecutive years.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the magnitude of interannual biomass change correlates with species composition turnover.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef and each pair of consecutive years, compute:  a) Δbiomass = |total_biomass_year_t+1 − total_biomass_year_t|.  b) Jaccard turnover = 1 − (size of species intersection / size of species union) for years t and t+1. 2. Build a dataset with one row per reef‐year‐pair containing Δbiomass and turnover. 3. Fit a mixed‐effects regression: turnover ~ Δbiomass + (1|id_reef). 4. Evaluate whether the slope for Δbiomass is significantly positive. 5. Scatterplot of turnover vs Δbiomass with fitted line and confidence band.","deliverables":"1. Regression model summary showing relationship between biomass change and turnover.  2. Scatterplot with regression line.  3. Table of descriptive statistics (mean Δbiomass, mean turnover).  4. Interpretation of correlation between biomass fluctuations and species turnover."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Protected reefs exhibit lower interannual variability in species richness compared to unprotected reefs, demonstrating stabilization through protection.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare the standard deviation of species richness across years between protection statuses.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef, compute species richness per year.  2. For each reef, calculate the standard deviation (SD) of richness across all available years.  3. Merge reef‐level protection_status (e.g. 'protected' vs 'unprotected').  4. Use a two‐sample t‐test or Wilcoxon test to compare the distribution of richness‐SD between protection statuses.  5. Plot boxplots of richness‐SD by protection_status.","deliverables":"1. Table of mean and SD of richness‐SD for each protection_status.  2. Results of statistical test (test statistic and p‐value).  3. Boxplot figure.  4. Interpretation of whether protection reduces richness variability."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"After the 2014–2016 marine heatwave, protected reefs show a steeper positive recovery slope in total biomass compared to unprotected reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Estimate post‐anomaly biomass recovery rates by protection status using piecewise regression.",  
      "steps":"1. Define three periods: pre‐anomaly (≤2013), anomaly (2014–2016), post‐anomaly (≥2017).  2. Compute total biomass per reef‐year and attach protection_status.  3. Create a numeric time variable (year) and an indicator for post‐anomaly period.  4. Fit a mixed‐effects piecewise model: biomass ~ year + post_indicator:year + protection_status:post_indicator:year + (1|id_reef).  5. Test whether the interaction term protection_status×post_indicator×year is positive.  6. Plot biomass trajectories over years by protection_status with fitted piecewise lines.","deliverables":"1. Model summary showing recovery slope differences.  2. Line plot of biomass vs year with separate curves for protected vs unprotected.  3. Table of estimated slopes pre‐ and post‐anomaly by protection_status.  4. Interpretation of differential recovery."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Spatial nestedness among reef species assemblages has increased from 2010 to 2020, with species‐poor reefs becoming more like subsets of species‐rich reefs over time.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compute and compare nestedness (e.g. NODF) of reef–species presence‐absence matrices in early vs late periods.",  
      "steps":"1. Define two time periods: early (2010–2014) and late (2016–2020).  2. For each period, build a presence‐absence matrix of species (columns) by reef (rows) aggregating across years in that period.  3. Using a nestedness algorithm (e.g. NODF from the bipartite or vegan R package, or python nestedness package), compute nestedness score for each period matrix.  4. Use a null model (randomize species occurrences preserving row/column sums, 999 replicates) to assess significance of observed nestedness in each period.  5. Compare early vs late nestedness scores and test whether the increase is significant.","deliverables":"1. Nestedness scores (observed and null‐model mean) for early and late periods, with p‐values.  2. Bar chart comparing observed nestedness over time.  3. Interpretation of whether nestedness has strengthened."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Temporal trends in species richness differ significantly among biogeographic regions of the Gulf, with some regions gaining and others losing species from 2010 to 2020.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test for region×year interactions in species richness trajectories.",  
      "steps":"1. Assign each reef to its region (field region).  2. Compute species richness per reef‐year.  3. Fit a mixed‐effects model: richness ~ year * region + (1|id_reef).  4. Test significance of region×year interaction terms.  5. For regions with significant slopes, extract and plot fitted richness trends over time by region.","deliverables":"1. Model summary with region×year coefficients and p‐values.  2. Line plot of predicted richness vs year for each region.  3. Table of per‐region annual slope estimates.  4. Interpretation of biogeographic differences."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The coefficient of variation (CV) of biomass across transects within each reef decreases over time, indicating greater biomass homogenization within reefs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether within‐reef biomass heterogeneity among transects declines from 2010 to 2020.",  
      "steps":"1. For each reef‐year, gather biomass per transect.  2. Compute mean and standard deviation of transect biomass, then CV = sd/mean per reef‐year.  3. Fit a linear mixed model: CV ~ year + (1|id_reef).  4. Test whether the slope for year is significantly negative.  5. Plot CV vs year with fitted regression line.","deliverables":"1. Mixed‐effects model summary for CV trend.  2. Scatterplot of CV vs year with regression.  3. Table of average CV in early vs late five‐year blocks.  4. Interpretation of within‐reef homogenization."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The share of total biomass contributed by the top decile of transects within each region‐year increases over time, reflecting increasing skew in biomass distribution across transects.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess changes in biomass distribution skew by quantifying the top 10% transect contribution to region‐level biomass.",  
      "steps":"1. For each region and year, list all transects with their total biomass.  2. Identify the top 10% of transects by biomass in that region‐year.  3. Compute share = (sum biomass of top decile) / (sum biomass of all transects in region‐year).  4. Build a dataset of share vs year for each region.  5. Fit a mixed model or fixed‐effect model: share ~ year + (1|region).  6. Test whether the slope year is positive overall or per region.  7. Plot share vs year with trend lines.","deliverables":"1. Model summary for biomass share trend.  2. Plot of top‐decile share over time by region.  3. Table of share in 2010, 2015, 2020 for each region.  4. Interpretation of distribution skew trends."  
    }  
  }  
]}
